From 7a265f7d5978c6297661808f656047df34c355d7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 12:15:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Sampling profiler: Settings > CPU monitor > Profile sends
 where the CPU spends its time to the computer

A measurement version's tool for the question "what takes the CPU in a big song" on the device itself, with its
caches, which the emulator doesn't model (the emulator showed the idle 87 % is the audio routine filling free time
with blocks of 4-8 samples; the device's per-call cost is unknown).

- OS timer 1 (unused so far) interrupts 1000 times a second at the lowest priority (30), so it's never taken inside
  another interrupt's handler, only from the main program: then irq_handler has pushed the interrupted address and
  SPSR onto the empty IRQ stack, and the handler reads them at irq_stack_end - 4 and - 8. No change to the interrupt
  entry.
- Each sample: that address and what the CPU was doing, from stores the firmware makes anyway: the task
  (TaskManager::runTask(), nesting through yield()), inside AudioEngine::routine(), which output renders
  (Song::renderAudio()). A weight counts the timer periods since the last sample: renderOutput() runs with interrupts
  disabled, so the samples of that time come where they're enabled again, and stand for all of it.
- Because of that, each output's render time is also measured exactly around renderOutput() (OS timer 0) while
  profiling, and sent once a second.
- The samples go into a ring of 1024 (one writer, one reader, no locks) and out from the CPU monitor's task as SysEx
  on USB MIDI port 3, like the CPU monitor's numbers, only while the buffer keeps 1 KB free for notes: 32 samples a
  message (~8 KB/s), the tasks' and outputs' names every 2 s, the output times each second. Formats in
  profiler_core.h (host-testable: ring and encoders).
- Settings > CPU monitor: Off, On, Alerts, Profile (Profile = On plus the profiler).
- Off, it costs the context stores and a flag test per output; on, ~150 cycles a sample and two timer reads per
  output.

